One more consideration: is there a subtlety where `pskb_expand_head` with nhead=8, ntail=0 might not free the old head? pskb_expand_head always allocates new data and copies, then frees old head if dataref==1 (skb_release_data → skb_free_head → kfree). With dataref > 1 (cloned), it just drops the reference. So stale `wh` is either freed memory (UAF read of up to hdrlen bytes → info copied into TX frame) or aliased shared memory. Either way, the code comment at line 885 `if (wh != &tr->wh)` shows the author knew `wh` could differ from the new location — and uses it as memmove SOURCE regardless. The value `hdrlen` was computed from stale-but-same-value frame_control (value read pre-expand at line 859 is fine), so the read length is fixed; the read source `wh` is the dangling pointer. Classic variant of the seed's "stale pointer dereference after head reallocating helper".

Note the seed was a READ+WRITE; here it's a READ (memmove source + frame_control check at 881). Line 881 `ieee80211_is_data_qos(wh->frame_control)` reads 2 bytes from freed memory; result changes hdrlen → out-of-bounds-ish read length from freed slab into TXed frame (info leak of freed slab bytes, though only difference ±2 bytes). Impact modest but the mechanism matches.

